WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.197
Three quick questions.

2
00:00:05.197 --> 00:00:07.598
Is one a prime number?

3
00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:09.059
Is zero even?

4
00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:11.460
Does zero divide zero?

5
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.383
Ask a room of people

6
00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:15.705
and you get different answers

7
00:00:15.705 --> 00:00:17.727
all given with confidence.

8
00:00:17.727 --> 00:00:18.926
In mathematics

9
00:00:18.926 --> 00:00:21.922
that argument is not allowed to happen.

10
00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:23.570
A definition decides.

11
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.426
So, is zero even?

12
00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:29.080
Pause and decide.

13
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.500
A definition is a test.

14
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.540
Give it any object of the right kind

15
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.620
and it answers yes or no.

16
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.780
Here is the test for even

17
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.780
run on the integers from

18
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.460
minus three to four.

19
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.928
Minus two, zero, two and four pass.

20
00:00:49.928 --> 00:00:55.106
Minus three, minus one, one and three fail.

21
00:00:55.106 --> 00:00:59.930
So zero is even: zero is two times zero.

22
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.021
Every integer lands in exactly one group.

23
00:01:04.021 --> 00:01:07.305
There is no answer like it depends

24
00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:08.400
or sort of.

25
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.810
A good definition has four parts.

26
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.096
Take this one.

27
00:01:13.096 --> 00:01:15.583
It starts with the category

28
00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:17.470
a positive integer n.

29
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.956
Then the term being defined

30
00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:21.500
a perfect square.

31
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.889
Then the word provided

32
00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:26.193
which means exactly when.

33
00:01:26.193 --> 00:01:28.409
And last, the condition

34
00:01:28.498 --> 00:01:30.802
there is an integer m with

35
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:32.752
n equal to m squared.

36
00:01:32.752 --> 00:01:33.905
Nine passes

37
00:01:33.905 --> 00:01:37.450
nine dots make a three by three square.

38
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.315
Compare: a number is large

39
00:01:40.315 --> 00:01:43.761
provided it is much bigger than one.

40
00:01:43.761 --> 00:01:45.252
Is fifty large?

41
00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:46.742
Nobody can say.

42
00:01:46.742 --> 00:01:48.605
That is not a test

43
00:01:48.605 --> 00:01:51.120
so it is not a definition.

44
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.581
We also need names for the numbers we use.

45
00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:59.277
The natural numbers, N: zero, one, two

46
00:01:59.277 --> 00:02:00.293
and so on.

47
00:02:00.293 --> 00:02:04.174
In this course, zero is a natural number.

48
00:02:04.174 --> 00:02:07.500
The integers, Z, add the negatives.

49
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.057
The rational numbers, Q

50
00:02:10.057 --> 00:02:12.264
are fractions a over b

51
00:02:12.264 --> 00:02:15.299
with integers on top and bottom

52
00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:16.770
and b not zero.

53
00:02:16.862 --> 00:02:19.345
Zero point three seven five

54
00:02:19.437 --> 00:02:21.001
is three eighths

55
00:02:21.001 --> 00:02:23.668
and seven is seven over one.

56
00:02:23.668 --> 00:02:27.530
The real numbers, R, fill the whole line.

57
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.020
We assume that integers can be added

58
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.311
subtracted and multiplied.

59
00:02:33.311 --> 00:02:36.960
But we assume nothing about dividing them.

60
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.760
That is the next word we define.

61
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.805
Here it is.

62
00:02:41.805 --> 00:02:47.397
b divides a, provided a equals b times c

63
00:02:47.397 --> 00:02:50.060
for some integer c.

64
00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.981
In a picture: a dots fill b equal rows.

65
00:02:53.981 --> 00:02:57.553
Forty-two dots make six rows of seven.

66
00:02:57.553 --> 00:03:00.300
Forty-two is six times seven

67
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.589
and seven is an integer.

68
00:03:02.589 --> 00:03:04.970
So six divides forty-two.

69
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.398
Now try forty dots in six rows.

70
00:03:08.398 --> 00:03:12.257
Six full rows of six use thirty-six dots

71
00:03:12.257 --> 00:03:14.462
and four are left over.

72
00:03:14.462 --> 00:03:17.677
They cannot fill a seventh column

73
00:03:17.677 --> 00:03:19.790
two places stay empty.

74
00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.611
In symbols: six times c equals forty needs

75
00:03:24.718 --> 00:03:27.805
c equal to twenty over three

76
00:03:27.805 --> 00:03:30.466
which is not an integer.

77
00:03:30.466 --> 00:03:33.660
So six does not divide forty.

78
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.612
Negative numbers are allowed.

79
00:03:36.612 --> 00:03:39.148
Seven divides minus fifty-six

80
00:03:39.148 --> 00:03:41.520
with c equal to minus eight.

81
00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.506
Zero needs care.

82
00:03:43.506 --> 00:03:45.754
Every b divides zero

83
00:03:45.754 --> 00:03:48.821
because zero is b times zero.

84
00:03:48.821 --> 00:03:51.172
But zero divides five?

85
00:03:51.172 --> 00:03:54.648
Zero times c is zero for every c

86
00:03:54.648 --> 00:03:55.874
never five.

87
00:03:55.874 --> 00:03:56.590
So no.

88
00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.759
And zero divides zero is true

89
00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:02.110
c equal to zero works.

90
00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:03.858
A common slip.

91
00:04:03.858 --> 00:04:06.861
Three divides twelve is a sentence.

92
00:04:06.861 --> 00:04:08.613
It is true or false

93
00:04:08.613 --> 00:04:11.283
like three is less than twelve.

94
00:04:11.283 --> 00:04:14.370
Twelve over three is a number: four.

95
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.520
So writing three divides twelve

96
00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.958
equals four mixes the two.

97
00:04:19.958 --> 00:04:22.036
And the order matters.

98
00:04:22.036 --> 00:04:24.655
Four divides twenty is true

99
00:04:24.655 --> 00:04:26.371
four rows of five.

100
00:04:26.371 --> 00:04:29.080
Twenty divides four is false.

101
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.991
Now a first proof.

102
00:04:31.991 --> 00:04:36.263
If d divides a, and d divides b

103
00:04:36.263 --> 00:04:39.500
then d divides a plus b.

104
00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.127
Unwrap the definitions.

105
00:04:42.127 --> 00:04:45.690
a is d times x, and b is d times y

106
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.253
with a new letter for each integer.

107
00:04:49.253 --> 00:04:52.222
Twelve is three rows of four

108
00:04:52.222 --> 00:04:55.290
fifteen is three rows of five.

109
00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.945
Name the goal: an integer c

110
00:04:58.945 --> 00:05:02.957
with a plus b equal to d times c.

111
00:05:02.957 --> 00:05:04.020
Compute.

112
00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.088
a plus b is d x plus d y

113
00:05:07.088 --> 00:05:10.275
which is d times x plus y.

114
00:05:10.275 --> 00:05:14.760
Side by side, the rows still line up

115
00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.120
three rows of nine.

116
00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:21.012
Last, check: x plus y is an integer

117
00:05:21.012 --> 00:05:25.047
because a sum of integers is an integer.

118
00:05:25.047 --> 00:05:27.310
So d divides a plus b.

119
00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:30.544
That is the pattern for this course.

120
00:05:30.544 --> 00:05:32.313
Unwrap each definition

121
00:05:32.386 --> 00:05:34.376
with a new letter for every

122
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.924
integer it promises.

123
00:05:35.924 --> 00:05:37.030
Name the goal.

124
00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:37.693
Compute.

125
00:05:37.693 --> 00:05:41.010
And check that what you found is an integer.

126
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.146
Try it yourself

127
00:05:43.146 --> 00:05:46.718
if d divides a and d divides b

128
00:05:46.718 --> 00:05:51.294
show that d divides three a minus two b.

129
00:05:51.294 --> 00:05:52.745
In part two

130
00:05:52.745 --> 00:05:57.433
the same pattern proves facts about even

131
00:05:57.433 --> 00:06:00.000
odd and prime numbers.
